#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""The sandbox's MCP gateway: user-selected stdio MCP servers, next to the agent.

A user can choose to run an MCP server *in the sandbox* (location ``sandbox``)
instead of behind the platform relay: it then sees the task's ``localhost``,
its files and the sandbox's browser. The trusted host decides which servers a
member may use; this program runs them and presents them to the member's agent
CLI as one Streamable HTTP MCP server, ``sandbox``, the way the relay presents
the remote ones as ``session``.

Three identities, so that a server, which is code the user chose but nobody
reviewed, can affect only its own member:

- **The admission** says which servers a member's slot may use. The host writes
  it as root (``admit``), into a root-owned directory; nothing below can change
  it. It names the SHA-256 of the member's MCP credential, the token the
  member's CLI already sends to the relay, so one header reaches both servers;
  the credential itself is never written here.
- **The gateway** (``agent-mcp``, uid 10003) serves the fixed address
  ``http://127.0.0.1:47801/mcp``, so Codex's root-owned allowlist names it
  once. It runs no server. For each request it finds the admission that
  matches the bearer token and forwards the call to that slot's runner.
- **A slot's runner** (``agent-mcp-<slot>``, uid 12001 + slot) runs that
  slot's admitted servers as stdio children and nothing else, and answers only
  the gateway, as the kernel reports the peer (``SO_PEERCRED``). A server can
  reach its own runner and its sibling servers, which are its member's; it
  cannot write an admission, signal the gateway or reach another slot.

None of them is a member's UID, so no member's turn-end reap stops them. The
host starts the runner and the gateway, which are idempotent, after writing an
admission. An admission written before the container last started is ignored:
whoever held its token is gone with that container.

Tools are presented as ``<alias>__<tool>``, as the relay names them. Only tools
are forwarded; the runner offers the servers no client capabilities (roots,
sampling, elicitation), so they never ask the agent anything. A server starts
when it is admitted, so the CLI's first tool list does not wait for an
install; it stops when it has served no call for the idle timeout and has none
running, or when its admission is withdrawn.

A server's arguments and environment may name ``${CLOUD_AGENT_BROWSER_CDP}``,
replaced at start with the sandbox browser's CDP URL (``cloud-agent-browser
start``, which also starts the runner's download courier).

Installs run with npm's ``ignore-scripts`` on. The host has already refused a
package without an exact version.

It runs isolated (``-I``), like the other helpers in this image, and uses the
standard library only.
"""

